MetaWrap2 v=2.1.4
	`metawrap2 score`, a progress heartbeat, a low-utilisation warning, verified multi-connection
		downloads, a GPU check before learned binners, benchmark arms that run another pipeline,
		`--preset slow` renamed `--preset gpu`, CheckM2 as the default bin scorer, aligner indexes
		reused safely, empty bin folders skipped in `dereplicate`, a real-sample benchmark on the
		metaWRAP 1 paper's datasets, and documentation layout changes.
	New - `metawrap2 score` for directories of genomes
		`metawrap2 score -o OUT bins_a bins_b ...` runs CheckM2 (or CheckM1 with `--checkm1`), GUNC and
			the MIMAG tags, writes the same per-genome table as `bin_refinement`, and prints one comparison
			table. Use it to compare bin sets from different pipelines with one scorer; metaWRAP 1 refines
			with CheckM1, which disagrees with CheckM2 most for near-complete bins.
		`-x` sets the FASTA extension (metaWRAP 1 writes `.fa`). Genomes are symlinked into the output,
			so the input directories are not written to. See docs/score.md.
	New - progress heartbeat
		After 60 s without output, a step prints its elapsed time and the tool's last line, so a long
			step with no output (COMEBin training, SemiBin2 clustering) can be told from a hung one. Screen only;
			the log files are unchanged. `heartbeat_seconds` under [settings] or `--heartbeat-seconds N`, 0 to
			turn it off.
	New - low-utilisation warning during a step
		On Linux, a step that has run for five minutes with less than a third of its thread grant busy
			prints a warning once. More threads will not speed it up; the warning suggests `-j` to run
			other samples on the idle cores. `low_utilisation_after_seconds` and `low_utilisation_fraction` under
			[settings]. The `-j` suggestion after a run now uses the same threshold (was a quarter).
	New - verified downloads; multi-connection downloads with aria2c
		A download goes to `.part`, is checked, and is renamed only if it passes: md5 when one is
			published, otherwise a test decompression. Size alone is not accepted. A failed file is deleted before the retry. Errors say which
			check failed.
		With aria2c on PATH, files are fetched over 8 connections, several times faster from ENA;
			otherwise curl, with the same checks. `METAWRAP2_ARIA2C=` (empty) forces curl.
		`install-db` uses the same downloader, so each database archive is test-decompressed before it
			is reported installed.
	New - GPU check before SemiBin2, VAMB and COMEBin
		Before a learned binner runs on a GPU, MetaWrap2 reports the card, its utilisation, free memory
			and other processes on it. A `--device cuda:N` the machine does not have stops the run before
			the step. A busy card prints a warning and the step runs; `--gpu-strict` (or
			`[limits] gpu_strict`) stops instead. Without a driver it prints a note; `--device cpu` skips
			the check.
	New - bin_refinement scores bin sets in parallel
		The hybrid bin sets are scored with CheckM2 several at a time when memory allows: `--checkm-jobs N`,
			default as many as fit at 24 GB per CheckM2 call, or with `--checkm1` 16 GB per CheckM1 call
			on the reduced tree and 40 GB with `--full-tree` (1 = one at a time). Bins are assigned to
			sets before scoring starts, so results match a sequential run. pplacer is capped at 8
			threads, past which it gained little. Three-binner refinement: CheckM2 730 s -> 539 s, final
			bins identical; CheckM1 on the full tree 1,969 s -> 798 s with identical stats.
		Figure: docs/figures/refine_speedup.png.
	New - `--checkm2-ttable`
		`--checkm2-ttable {4,11}` runs CheckM2 under one translation table instead of both; table 4 won
			for 8 of 6,778 bins in 45 CheckM2 reports.
	New - benchmark arms can run another pipeline
		A variant in benchmarks/configurations.toml can declare an `external` command (with its conda
			env, where its bins land, and its version) instead of `args`. It gets the same inputs, samples
			and budget as the MetaWrap2 arms, is measured the same way, and its bins are scored by the
			same scorer. Configuration errors are reported at load time, naming the arm. A commented
			metaWRAP 1.3.0 example is in the file.
	Changed - `--preset slow` renamed `--preset gpu`
		`slow` is still accepted and prints that it is the old name; study sheets accept it too. Same binners: metaBAT2 + SemiBin2 + COMEBin.
		`--preset gpu` trains SemiBin2 and COMEBin with `--device cuda` unless `--device` is given; the
			other presets keep `--device cpu`. COMEBin did not finish three CAMI2 samples on CPU in 280
			CPU-hours. If binners are named explicitly the preset and its device default are ignored.
	Changed - bin_refinement scores with CheckM2 by default (was CheckM1)
		`bin_refinement`, `reassemble_bins` and `binning --run-checkm` score bins with CheckM2, as
			`metawrap2 score` already did. CheckM2 needs less memory than CheckM1 on its full genome tree
			(pplacer holds about 34 GB per call), is faster, and scored the final bins in the MetaWrap2
			benchmarks. `--checkm1` scores with CheckM1 in `bin_refinement` and `binning --run-checkm`;
			it needs `metawrap2 install-db checkm`, and a `--checkm1` run without that database stops at
			the start and names the command. `--checkm2` is still accepted and changes nothing.
		The score cache is per scorer and CheckM1 tree (`work_files/checkm_scores.checkm2.json`,
			`.checkm1_reduced.json`, `.checkm1.json` for the full tree), and `work_files/scorer.txt`
			records it. A `--resume` with another scorer or tree stops; for a run without that file the
			scorer is read from the `.stats.tsv` lineage column, and CheckM1 is taken as the full tree.
	Changed - `--checkm1` uses CheckM1's reduced tree; `--full-tree` added, `--quick` hidden
		With `--checkm1`, `bin_refinement` and `binning --run-checkm` pass `--reduced_tree`, budgeted at
			16 GB per call and per pplacer thread. `--full-tree` uses the full genome tree (40 GB), as
			2.1.3 did. The pplacer-crash fallback to the reduced tree applies only with `--full-tree`.
		`--quick` is no longer in the help; it is still accepted and prints that the reduced tree is now
			the default. `--full-tree` under CheckM2 prints a note and is ignored.
	Changed - `reassemble_bins` scores with CheckM2 only
		`--checkm1` stops with "reassemble_bins scores bins with CheckM2 only, so --checkm1 is not
			available." `--checkm2` is accepted and changes nothing. CheckM1's `bin_qa_plot`
			(`reassembled_bins.png`) is no longer made; `reassembly_results.png` is unchanged.
	Changed - `benchmark score` scores with CheckM2 by default
		`--checkm1` (deprecated) scores with CheckM1 on the full tree; `--checkm2` is accepted. The
			per-set score cache is named after the scorer (`<bin set>.checkm_scores.checkm2.json`,
			`.checkm1.json`), and a `.stats.tsv` written by the other scorer is scored again and not
			counted, so earlier CheckM1 scores are not read as CheckM2 scores.
		Install: `quickstart` builds `metawrap2-checkm2`, and `quickstart --binning-only --databases`
			fetches `checkm2`, `gunc` and `host` (was `checkm` and `host`). CheckM1's data is opt-in.
			`doctor` counts a missing `metawrap2-checkm2` as a failure for bin_refinement and
			reassemble_bins and checks CHECKM2DB in place of CHECKM_DB; `doctor --fix` rebuilds the env.
	Fixed - full `quickstart` built no environments
		Without `--binning-only` or `--modules`, quickstart passed an empty list to `install-env`, which
			exited with "specify one or more modules, or --all". It now passes every module environment
			plus `checkm2` and `learned`.
	Fixed - aligner indexes reused when current, built under a lock
		`align_read_sets` built the index unconditionally, so `--resume` re-indexed an assembly it had
			already indexed, and parallel mappings against one reference all wrote the same index file.
		An index is now reused when every file it consists of (`aligners.INDEX_FILES`) exists, is non-empty
			and is newer than the reference, and a marker written after a successful build records the
			same index command. A lock around check-and-build makes parallel mappings take turns; the
			first builds the index and the others reuse it. The marker also stops a short-read minimap2 index from being
			reused for long reads, which need a different preset.
	Fixed - `plot_binning_results` with an empty first bin set
		The label loop left `x_pos` unset for an empty set, so the plot raised NameError and stopped
			`bin_refinement` after CheckM and GUNC had run. The label now goes at x = 0.
	Fixed - bin_refinement test with long temporary paths
		The warning it checks is wrapped by the banner, so a long path split the matched phrase. The
			test now strips the banner before matching.
	Fixed - `dereplicate` skips empty bin folders
		A sample whose binning or refinement found nothing leaves an empty folder, which is common in a
			cohort. Empty folders are now skipped with a warning; only an all-empty input is an error.
	Fixed - CAMI2 skin sample composition in benchmarks/datasets.toml
		The comment said the deposit's airskinurogenital samples are ordered airways, skin, urogenital.
			They are interleaved. The per-sample assignment is now listed (skin: 1, 13-20, 28; airways: 4,
			7-12, 23, 26, 27; urogenital: 0, 2, 3, 5, 6, 21, 22, 24, 25), measured against each site's gold
			standard and the deposit's abundance files. `cami2_skin` (samples 17-19) was already correct.
	New - real-sample benchmark on four datasets
		Gut (50 MetaHIT samples) and water (36 Baltic Sea samples) from the metaWRAP 1 paper, one
		co-assembly each; one deeply sequenced Hadza gut sample (ERR7745335, published assembly); a
		three-sample faecal-transplant series (one co-assembly). All final sets scored with CheckM2 and
		GUNC. Clean MAGs (wall-clock, binning + refinement):
			  pipeline            gut 50        water 36      gut 1         gut 3
			  metaWRAP 1          94 (36.5 h)   183 (98.5 h)  158 (15.4 h)  72 (5.0 h)
			  MetaWrap2 default   110 (1.5 h)   184 (2.7 h)   207 (3.0 h)   78 (2.2 h)
			  MetaWrap2 gpu       125 (6.4 h)   197 (13.3 h)  250 (9.5 h)   111 (4.7 h)
		On the single and three-sample sets, metaWRAP 1 kept 186 and 87 bins by CheckM1; 174 and 83 meet
		its thresholds under CheckM2.
		Figure: docs/figures/real_samples_binning.png.
	New - CAMI2 and CAMI3 gold-assembly benchmark scored by AMBER
		Each pipeline bins the dataset's gold assembly; bp-weighted F1 for metaWRAP 1 / very-fast / fast /
			default / gpu: CAMI2 gastrointestinal 0.67 / 0.54 / 0.67 / 0.75 / 0.85, oral 0.36 / 0.25 / 0.35 /
			0.39 / 0.53, skin 0.38 / 0.30 / 0.36 / 0.47 / 0.59; CAMI3 toy 0.21 / 0.16 / 0.21 / 0.23 /
			0.23. Purity is 0.98-1.00 for every pipeline. The metaWRAP 1 arm is its refinement over
			metaBAT2, MaxBin2 and CONCOCT.
		Bins >=90% complete and <=5% contaminated against the truth, metaWRAP 1 / very-fast / fast / default /
			gpu: gastrointestinal 115 / 84 / 109 / 135 / 179, oral 146 / 96 / 134 / 156 / 247, skin 101 / 82 /
			100 / 135 / 197, CAMI3 toy (prokaryote-only truth) 175 / 124 / 166 / 161 / 215.
		Figure: docs/figures/synthetic_benchmarks.png (all CAMI2 and CAMI3 results).
	Changed - documentation
		The README benchmark section is split into synthetic (CAMI2) and real samples. Release
			notes moved from docs/releases/ to releases/. Open items from the 2.1.3, functional-annotation
			and other-modules plans are in PLANNED_FEATURES.md. docs/modules.md is now one reference
			entry per module.
MetaWrap2 v=2.1.3
	CAMI2 oral and skin added to the metaWRAP 1 comparison, binning presets measured on oral,
		`metawrap2 report` (PDF and per-genome table), MIMAG tags, `kraken2 --bracken`, `annotate
		--eggnog`, static functional wheels, `assembly --keep-graph`, six more `install-db` databases, and
		fixes from user testing: three defects that stopped the default pipeline on a fresh install and
		two that changed results.
	Fixed - read_qc now reports read retention and warns below 70%
		With its defaults, fastp drops a read when more than 40% of its bases are below Q15 and trims nothing;
			trim-galore trims the low-quality tail and keeps the read. On modern data the two give similar
			results (the CAMI2 arm with fastp kept slightly more). On 2010-era 75 bp reads fastp discarded
			14,755,926 of 25,242,208 reads and shortened none, leaving 5.24 M pairs (trim-galore: 12.04 M).
		nonpareil then put the extra sequencing needed for 95% coverage at 20x. With trim-galore the same
			sample gives 4.06x, matching the published figure (0.837 coverage against a documented 0.844).
		read_qc now reports retention from the trimmer's own report: `12,621,104 pairs in -> 5,243,141 out
			(41.5%): 58.5% removed by quality filtering`. Below 70% it warns and suggests trim-galore.
			`--fastp-quality-trim` adds `--cut_front --cut_tail --cut_mean_quality 20`.
		The default trimmer is unchanged: changing it needs a measured arm, and the benchmark set has no
			low-quality dataset, the only case where the trimmers differ. The gap is recorded in
			benchmarks/datasets.toml.
	Fixed - reassemble_bins now scores the original bins
		The module picks the best of three candidates per bin: strict reassembly, permissive reassembly and
			the original. The original was staged as `<bin>.orig.fa` while CheckM runs with `-x fasta`, so it was
			never scored and `choose_best_bin` could only return a reassembly. The earlier `.fa` -> `.fasta`
			rename missed this call site.
		On 26 real bins, before -> after: strict 9 -> 7, permissive 17 -> 15, original kept 0 -> 4. So 4 of 26
			bins (15%) had been replaced by a worse genome, and the summary reported "0 bins were not improved by
			any reassembly".
		The run then failed with an IndexError in the plot after 62 minutes: `original_bins.stats.tsv` was
			header-only, and the label index `len(series)//4 - 1` is -1 on an empty list, here and in two places
			in the completion and contamination panels. All three are guarded, a failed figure is no longer fatal
			(bins and stats are written first), and the summary reports how many bins kept their original.
		The dry-run SPAdes preview matched only `(".fa", ".fasta")`, so a `.fna` bin folder that passes the
			module's validation showed no commands.
	Fixed - read_qc with FastQC 0.13.0, and FastQC thread count
		The pin was `fastqc >=0.12.1` with no upper bound. A fresh solve installed 0.13.0, whose argparse CLI
			has no short `-o`, so read_qc failed on every new install. `install-env --from-lock` worked (the
			lockfile pinned 0.12.1); the default path, used by quickstart, did not. read_qc now passes `--outdir`
			(accepted by both) and the pin is `fastqc >=0.12.1,<0.13`.
		FastQC now gets the same thread cap as trim-galore and fastp; with every available thread it warned on
			each run ("You can't use more than 4 threads per file").
	Fixed - KEGG modules table missing from the wheel
		`metawrap2/data/kegg/modules_table.tsv` was in the repository but not in the wheel. The package-data
			list in pyproject covered only the env files and was not extended when the KEGG table was added.
			`pathways` and `quantify_function` failed after a wheel install (the README's first install line),
			after the annotate step that feeds them. They worked under `pip install -e .`.
		The whole data directory is now globbed.
	Fixed - Pfam release not found when the config holds the HMM path
		`annotate --pfam` reported "Pfam release unknown" although relnotes.txt, which `install-db pfam`
			downloads, has RELEASE 38.2 on line 2. `release_from_notes` accepted a directory or the notes file,
			but install-db writes the HMM path into the config, so the function read through a 2.2 GB HMM looking
			for a release line. Given any other file, it now looks for the notes beside it, and it refuses
			candidates over 1 MiB.
	Fixed - `config show` reported BLASTDB_NAME as missing
		`BLASTDB_NAME nt` is a database name inside BLASTDB, not a path. `config.py` lists it in
			NON_PATH_DB_KEYS and `check_databases` respects that, but `config show` ran its own
			`os.path.exists("nt")`. It now uses the shared helper.
		`doctor` (resolving env names itself) and install-db (a second tar guard) had the same kind of
			duplicate and now call the shared helpers.
	Fixed - `doctor` and `check` include modules with no conda environment
		`quickstart` ended with "22 OK, 0 missing, 0 broken (of 22 modules)", and `diff_abundance` then failed
			on first use because scikit-bio was not installed. The table was built from the conda environment
			list, so the two modules with no environment were left out, and one of them needs an optional Python
			extra. `-h` lists 24 modules; the table had 22 rows.
		`doctor` now lists every module in the CLI and checks the extras through `importlib.metadata`. A
			missing extra shows as `diff_abundance (python extra) MISSING needs scikit-bio>=0.7.1: pip install
			'metawrap2[stats]'`. `check` has the same section and accepts `diff_abundance` and
			`quantify_function`, which it had rejected as unknown names because they have no conda environment.
			`quickstart` no longer says it installs "everything"; it lists what it builds and which extras it
			skips.
	Fixed - a killed `install-db` left its download running
		After a stopped run, two curl processes wrote to the same `.part` file: the orphan from the killed run
			and the new one resuming it with `-C -`. The file was corrupted and the next tar failed. Downloads
			now run in their own process group and are terminated with the command. A `.part` modified in the
			last two minutes is refused, by name, instead of resumed.
	Fixed - `annotate` exits non-zero when a tool fails on every bin
		With no Bakta database configured, all 26 Bakta runs failed and the module exited 0 with "ANNOTATE
			PIPELINE SUCCESSFULLY FINISHED", after printing 26 warnings. The database is now checked before
			anything runs, and a per-item loop in which every item fails fails the step: `Bakta failed on 26 of
			26 bins; no Bakta output was produced`. The dbCAN, GECCO, Pfam and KOfam loops have the same check.
	Fixed - log collapsing now shows progress lines
		COMEBin logs one line per epoch for 200 epochs. The collapser replaces numbers with <N>, so the step
			printed one line and then nothing for nine minutes. No uncollapsed copy was kept, so only COMEBin's
			internal log showed it had reached epoch 85. CheckM's "Finished processing 19 of 49 (38.78%) bins"
			was hidden the same way.
		After 20 repeats, a signature is now reprinted every 30 seconds with its current values and the
			suppressed count. A signature whose `n/total` keeps the same total while the count increases is
			treated as progress, and its closing summary shows the last value instead of the masked pattern. A
			40-minute step with 11,906 metaBAT2 warnings now prints about 80 lines (was 11,906 or 1).
		Also: each step prints the path of its live log when it starts, not only when it fails. A standalone
			module run now prints the cost line (`Ns wall, X GB peak memory, Y cores busy`), which was printed
			only for steps run by `run`. It shows, for example, when most of a large allocation is idle.
	Fixed - `run --example` uses the default preset and 70/10
		The generated sheet had `binning = "--metabat2 --maxbin2"` under a comment calling it "the defaults".
			That is the `fast` tier; the default is metaBAT2 + SemiBin2. The README table gives 41 clean MAGs for
			fast and 53 for the default, about 23% fewer. The same block had `bin_refinement = "-c 50 -x 10"`
			instead of the documented 70/10.
		The sheet now emits `--preset %s % DEFAULT_PRESET` and `-c 70 -x 10`, so it follows PRESETS, and it no
			longer pins `steps`.
	Fixed - `cohort` warns when a sheet's `steps` leaves out cohort steps
		With the sheet from `run --example`, `cohort` dropped `coverage` and `dereplicate`, the two steps that
			produce its cross-sample depth table and its non-redundant catalogue (in place of per-sample bins).
			It now warns, naming the missing steps and how to restore them, as it already did when overriding
			`coassemble = true` under cohort.
		Steps are now resolved after the mode is known, so a sheet that pins `coverage` under cohort keeps it
			(it was dropped with "co-assembling, so coverage has nothing to do"). `--example` is mode-aware, so
			`cohort --example | cohort`, the README's starting point, no longer warns.
	Fixed - nonpareil row labels for read_qc output
		`usage.md` documents `nonpareil -r READ_QC/*/final_pure_reads_1.fastq`. read_qc puts the sample name
			in the directory, so all these files have the same basename and the command failed on a sample-name
			collision. When basenames collide, row labels now fall back to the parent directory. Files already
			named per sample keep their names, and a collision the directory does not resolve is still an error.
	Fixed - `status` lists steps in execution order
		It sorted them alphabetically (assembly, bin_refinement, binning, quant_bins, read_qc,
			reassemble_bins).
	Added - CI jobs for fresh solves, wheel installs and an end-to-end run
		On the unpatched tree the suite gave 178 passed, 3 skipped, while four of twenty-four modules could
			not run. The unit tests did not exercise the conditions behind them: an environment solved fresh from
			an uncapped pin, a wheel install instead of an editable one, a real CheckM run over a directory of
			candidates, a real tarball, a machine with a second conda installation, and the path format
			install-db writes.
		The jobs: a scheduled job that solves the environment yamls fresh (not --from-lock) and runs the
			read-level step on the committed fixture; a per-PR job that builds the wheel, installs it into a
			clean venv, and runs every module's --help plus a real `pathways` from that install; and a scheduled
			end-to-end run of the fixture through `metawrap2 run` that checks the outputs exist. The first two
			would have caught the FastQC and wheel-data defects above.
	Changed - nonpareil figures in docs/modules.md name their trimmer and kernel
		docs/modules.md quoted nonpareil coverage of 84.4%/74.6%/89.4% for the test cohort without naming the
			QC. With the current default those samples give 0.65. The trimmer change left 75 bp reads instead of
			34 bp, so `--kernel auto` switches to the k-mer kernel, and the two kernels differ by more than 30
			points on this sample. The figures now name their trimmer and kernel and give the current default's
			value.
	Fixed - environment lookup with more than one conda installation
		Every tool was reported broken. `conda_env_exists` checks every conda installation it can see (`conda
			env list` reads `~/.conda/environments.txt`, which is machine-wide), while `doctor` ran tools with
			`mamba run -n`, which resolves only against the active root. `quickstart` found 23 environments from
			an unrelated miniforge, built nothing ("already exists"), then reported 22 of 22 modules BROKEN in
			the same run.
		An environment is now resolved to a prefix once, by `config.env_prefix`, and every probe runs `mamba
			run -p <prefix>`, with `-n` as the fallback when no prefix resolves. The probe keeps the subprocess
			output, so a missing prefix reports `env prefix does not exist: <path>` instead of `not on PATH` (a
			tester spent fifty minutes on the old message). An environment outside the active root is reported as
			such.
		`config.env_dirs` now matches its docstring: the hardcoded `~/miniforge3/envs`-style guesses are used
			only when no env manager answers. Added unconditionally, they caused the collision even when `conda
			env list` was clean.
		`install-env`'s skip message names where it found the environment, and it counts unique environments
			instead of modules (11, not 23; the list repeated `metawrap2-reads` five times).
	Fixed - `install-db` for amrfinder, genomad, checkv, bakta and gtdbtk
		`install-db` knew 13 names; the README table had 18. The five missing ones blocked `amr`, `viruses`,
			`annotate --bakta` and `classify_bins --gtdbtk` on the documented path, with no documented
			alternative.
		Each now installs by name with the tool's own downloader inside its environment, as CheckM2 already
			did: `amrfinder_update --database`, `genomad download-database`, `checkv download_database`,
			`bakta_db download` (light variant under `--small`, 1.3 GB against 30 GB) and GTDB-Tk's
			`download-db.sh`. Each writes the resolved path to the key its module reads (`GTDBTK_DATA_PATH` for
			GTDB-Tk, not `GTDBTK_DB`) and leaves a marker so a re-run skips it.
	Fixed - `install-db card` rejected the archive's `.` member
		The install always failed, which blocked `amr --rgi`. CARD's tarball has a `.` root member. A separate
			guard tested `destination.startswith(realpath(target) + os.sep)`; `realpath` normalises `<target>/.`
			to `<target>`, which is not a strict prefix of itself, so the guard rejected the archive's root and
			the install aborted.
		That guard is removed. CARD now extracts through the same `_extract`/`_escapes` as the other
			databases, which check for a leading `/` and for `..` components and accept `.`. A test uses a
			tarball with a `.` member.
	Fixed - `install-db` writes each config key when its database finishes
		The config was written once, after the whole pool completed. In one report nine databases were
			started, four finished (17 GB on disk), the run was stopped, and the config had none of them, so
			`check` reported them missing.
		Each key is now written when its database finishes. The end-of-run summary still prints every key and
			writes only those not already present.
	Fixed - a failed database install prints the installer's output
		`FAILED: dbcan` was the only diagnostic; the subprocess's error text went to a stdout shared with two
			other databases' progress bars. The failure was transient: the same command worked when re-run by
			hand.
		Each installer's output is kept in a ring buffer, the last 15 lines are printed under the FAILED line,
			and the run ends with `N database(s) failed: ... Re-run the same command to retry; completed
			databases are detected and skipped`.
	Fixed - `install-db` honours `--dry-run`
		`metawrap2 --dry-run install-db gtdbtk` would have downloaded ~110 GB, because the command called
			subprocess directly instead of through the runner that handles the flag. It now changes nothing (no
			download, extraction, mamba call or config write) and prints, per database, the command or URL, the
			destination, both sizes and the key it would set, then the totals and the same disk-headroom line as
			a real run.
		Presence checks also run under a dry run, so the plan says `already present, would skip` instead of
			planning a 90 GB re-download. A missing conda environment is a note under a dry run and still fatal
			otherwise.
	Fixed - size columns in `install-db --list`
		One column held four kinds of figure: a bare size, a download size, a download-and-unpacked pair, and
			"~20 GB + indexing"; only 4 of 13 rows gave the unpacked size. GUNC, which runs by default in
			`bin_refinement`, was listed at 6.7 GB and takes about 13 GB.
		There are now four aligned columns (download, on disk, `--small`, and which modules need it), with
			totals (~580 GB / ~596 GB / ~160 GB), a legend marking estimates, and a note that SingleM's
			metapackage downloads from Zenodo at 150-300 KB/s and takes 2-4 hours. The README table has the same
			two sizes.
	Fixed - `bin_refinement` stopped when one input bin set was empty
		A binner that finds nothing to cluster writes an empty folder and exits 0, but every input set
			had to contain bins, so `-A metabat2_bins -B semibin2_bins` failed whenever one binner made
			no bins. Empty sets are now dropped with a warning and the rest are refined (and relabelled
			binsA, binsB, ... in order). With one set left it is scored with CheckM and cut at -c/-x as
			a single-set run always was. With every set empty the run finishes with an empty final
			folder, a header-only stats table and a warning. A path that does not exist is still an
			error.
	Fixed - concurrent manifest writes under `cohort` or `run -j N`
		`Manifest.save()` wrote a fixed `manifest.json.tmp` and renamed it, so two threads saving at
			once raised `FileNotFoundError: .../manifest.json.tmp`. A user lost a 36-sample study at -j 8
			after 33 assemblies (recovered with `--resume`). A second race let `json.dump` serialise the
			manifest while another thread added a step.
		The temp path is now per writer (pid and thread), and a re-entrant lock covers updating and
			writing. Inputs and outputs are fingerprinted before the lock is taken, so hashing does not
			block other steps. A failed write removes its temp file and leaves the previous manifest.
	Fixed - `coverage --keep-bams` BAMs kept catalogue contig names
		With `-a LABEL=assembly`, `coverage` aligns against a catalogue whose contigs are named
			`LABEL~contig` and splits the depth table back to plain names, but kept BAMs stayed
			prefixed. SemiBin2, VAMB, COMEBin and CONCOCT read the BAM, matched no contigs against the
			plain assembly and produced no bins, with no error. Two users hit it.
		With one assembly the BAM headers are rewritten (`samtools reheader`, @SQ SN fields only). With
			several they are not, since contig names repeat between assemblies; the run warns, names
			the affected binners and prints the samtools command that selects one assembly.
	New - `binning --bams DIR`: bin from existing alignments
		`--depth` is refused for CONCOCT and the learned binners, which read alignments, and there was
			no way to pass them alignments. Splitting a cohort between a CPU machine (alignment) and a
			GPU machine (binning) was not possible.
		The BAMs are symlinked, not copied, and never deleted. Their headers are checked against the
			assembly first: no overlap is an error naming both first contigs; partial overlap is
			reported and allowed, since a catalogue slice covers more contigs than one assembly.
			`--bams` and `--depth` can be combined, in which case nothing is aligned.
	Fixed - `install-db` for a tool-owned database without its environment
		On a fresh machine `install-db checkm2` failed with `critical libmamba The given prefix does
			not exist`. The environment is checked first and the message names `metawrap2 install-env
			checkm2`. Applies to all five databases fetched by their own tool.
	Changed - the depth phase measures several BAMs at once
		`samtools depth` does not use many threads well: ~2 minutes per BAM using every thread of a large
			machine at ~25% load, 6h44m of `coverage` for 36 samples. Each BAM fills its own column, so
			up to 16 now run at once with the thread budget divided between them. Results are
			identical to the serial path, which a test checks. One BAM, or fewer than four threads,
			runs as before.
	Changed - MaxBin2's iteration limit is explicit
		`run_MaxBin.pl` was called without `-max_iteration`, so MaxBin2's default of 50 applied
			implicitly. Each iteration costs more with more contigs and samples: ~53 minutes on a
			418k-contig co-assembly of 36 samples, about 40 hours for 50. `--maxbin2-max-iteration`
			sets it, defaulting to 50, so no result changes.
	Changed - kept BAMs are indexed
		`--keep-bams` in `coverage` and `binning` now writes a `.bai` beside each sorted BAM.
	Changed - faster FASTQ validation
		`check_fastq` reads every input to catch a truncated gzip member, but counted lines in a Python
			loop: ~17 minutes of one core for a 21 GB + 23 GB pair. It now counts newlines in 8 MB
			binary chunks, through `pigz -dc` where installed. The whole stream is still read.
	Fixed - `bin_refinement` retries once when CheckM's pplacer crashes
		On three of six steps of a skin preset arm, CheckM failed after aligning all marker genes:
			pplacer died with `Dune__exe__Multiprocessing.Child_error(_)` at seven threads, and the step
			ended with `CheckM did not produce .../storage/bin_stats_ext.tsv`. The other three steps on
			the same host finished.
		The step now retries once with a single pplacer thread, after clearing the output and tmp
			directories so pplacer does not resume from a partial tree. If the retry also fails, the
			error is as before.
		One alignment of 40 (2,022 residues, nothing unusual in it) crashed pplacer on its own, at one
			thread, every time, but placed fine against CheckM's reduced reference tree. A third attempt
			therefore adds `--reduced_tree`, with a warning: the reduced tree can choose a different
			marker set, so that bin set's completeness and contamination are not strictly comparable
			with a full-tree run. Skipped under `--quick`, which already uses the reduced tree.
	Fixed - the default `cohort` could not run its binning step
		With the cohort coverage stage on, each sample's binning got `--depth <slice>`. The default
			binners are metaBAT2 + SemiBin2, and `binning` refuses `--depth` with SemiBin2, so a default
			`cohort` stopped at the first binning step after read QC, assembly and cohort-wide
			alignment. Reported from a 36-sample run.
		`coverage --split-bams` now writes one indexed BAM per assembly per sample under
			`<out>/bams/<label>/`, with that assembly's contig names, and `cohort` passes both the
			table (for metaBAT2) and the BAMs (for SemiBin2). `binning` accepts `--depth` and `--bams`
			together. The split reads the catalogue BAMs about once, by region through the index.
			Checked on a two-assembly catalogue: 38,372 + 38,428 of 76,800 records, none lost or
			duplicated, and `binning --bams` on the result produced metaBAT2 and CONCOCT bins.
	Fixed - FastTree treated every alignment as nucleotide
		`fasttree -nt -gtr` ran on every alignment, but both documented inputs (pangenome core-gene
			alignments and GTDB-Tk's MSA) are protein. FastTree does not fail: it reads every residue
			other than A, C, G and T as ambiguous and returns a tree from very little information.
		`read_alignment` now measures the type from the residue composition (90% of residues in the
			IUPAC nucleotide codes; fourteen amino-acid letters are also nucleotide codes, so a
			membership test would call protein DNA), and FastTree gets `-nt -gtr` or `-lg`. The summary
			records the model used. Unclear cases are treated as protein. IQ-TREE was not affected
			(`-m MFP` detects the type).
	Fixed - `--device cuda:N` did not pin SemiBin2 or VAMB to card N
		Only COMEBin takes a device index. SemiBin2 gets `--engine gpu` and VAMB `--cuda`, so both
			reduced `cuda:1` to "use a GPU" and torch used device 0. On a multi-GPU host a SemiBin2 arm
			asked for cuda:1 trained on cuda:0 for 1.5 hours, sharing it with another job, while the
			sampler on cuda:1 recorded 3 MiB.
		All three binners are now pinned with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES (`binning.gpu_pin`, using the
			existing and previously unused `limits.gpu_env`). A pinned child sees its card as device 0,
			so COMEBin is then given plain `-d cuda`. Bare `--device cuda` and `auto` are unchanged.
		It went unnoticed because on a single-GPU or idle multi-GPU host the bins and timings look
			normal. The regression tests check the environment each command gets, so they need no GPU.
		`--preset slow` help now says COMEBin needs a GPU.
	New - steps that use a GPU record their peak VRAM
		`rusage` does not see GPU memory. `run_measured` now samples `nvidia-smi --query-compute-apps`
			every 5 seconds while a step runs and keeps the most VRAM a single device held for the step's
			own process tree: per device, since the question is whether it fits on one card, and per
			process tree, so other jobs on the card are not counted. Modules run directly sample
			themselves the same way; under `run` only the driver samples. A step that used no GPU records
			no VRAM keys rather than a zero. Sampler failures are ignored so they cannot fail a step.
		The cost line shows `6.0 GB peak VRAM on 2 GPU(s)`, and the manifest has `peak_gpu_memory_bytes`
			and `gpu_devices`.
		First measurements, on NVIDIA GPUs: COMEBin 5.71 GB on both a 16,936-contig and a
			63,376-contig assembly (set by batch size), SemiBin2 0.41 GB rising to 2.75 GB over the same
			pair, VAMB 0.56 GB. The README has a new Hardware section with these and measured per-step
			RAM.
	Fixed - `benchmark fetch` treated a dataset as complete with one of three samples extracted
		The plan checked only that the archive was on disk with the right md5, so an interrupted
			extraction left `fetch` reporting nothing to do and `run` failing later on reads that were
			never unpacked. A verified archive with no sample directories is now reported as "downloaded
			but not extracted" and is extracted without downloading again. Found while fetching the oral
			samples.
	Fixed - line wrapping in the closing banner
		Three of the four lines in the final banner were malformed. Each line was padded to the box width
			before wrapping, so sentences broke mid-line and the right `*****` landed on the next line. Text is
			now wrapped to the inner width first, then centred and padded.
	Fixed - `config init` comments out all placeholder paths
		KRAKEN2_DB, BMTAGGER_DB, BLASTDB and TAXDUMP were written uncommented while the other eight keys were
			commented, so `config show` reported four databases as configured but missing, which needs a
			different action from "not configured". All placeholders are now commented, `config show` prints
			`[not set]`, and `metawrap2.toml.example` was regenerated.
	Fixed - `quickstart --skip-envs`
		Documented as "databases only", it printed the environment table, reported success, exited 1 and did
			not touch any database. It now reports database status, and in a non-interactive shell with no
			`--databases` it says nothing will be downloaded and names the option to pass. It does not prompt, so
			it still does not block under `sbatch`/CI.
		Skipped steps are numbered (`STEP 2/3: databases (skipped: --databases none)`), `--log-dir` is
			forwarded so the solver logs of a successful install can be kept, and the two pure-Python modules
			missing from the status table (diff_abundance, quantify_function) are listed as needing nothing.
	Fixed - progress output during environment builds
		Eleven conda solves ran for 29 minutes with no output between the banner and the result, and the
			per-env logs went to a temp directory, so there was nothing to tail. Each environment now prints when
			it starts and when it finishes, with elapsed time and a count over the real number of environments,
			and the step banner gives the measured duration.
	Fixed - `metawrap2 -v` shows the git commit in a checkout
		From a checkout one commit past 2.1.2 it printed `MetaWrap2 v=2.1.2`, and the README asks for this
			string in bug reports. It now prints `MetaWrap2 2.1.3 (git acbd3d1)` from a git checkout and the
			plain version from a wheel. The git call has a 2 s timeout and a silent fallback, so it cannot fail
			or hang.
	Fixed - README example step 3 used raw reads
		Step 3 of the three-command example used the raw `reads_1.fastq reads_2.fastq` after step 2 had used
			`READ_QC/final_pure_reads_*`, and dropped the inputs' `.gz`. As written, it either fails on a missing
			file or bins reads that were not QC'd or host-filtered. The positional arguments also sat next to a
			flag that takes a value, so it was unclear whether `--preset` was taking a path.
	Changed - upper bounds on pandas and numpy
		`pandas>=1.3` and `numpy>=1.20` resolved a fresh install to pandas 3.0.6 and numpy 2.5.4, two major
			versions with breaking changes that MetaWrap2 has not been run against. Upper bounds now state the
			tested range.
	Changed - Python 3.10+ and conda are stated in the install block
		The requirement was in `## Notes`, 250 lines below the install block. On stock RHEL 8 or older Ubuntu,
			`python3` is 3.9, and `pip install .` fails after downloading scipy, pyrodigal and seaborn, with
			nothing saying where to get a 3.10+ interpreter. The install block now gives the requirement, the
			Miniforge bootstrap, and whether conda is needed first.
		The sentence "`cohort` is `run --no-coassembly`" moved from usage.md to the README beside the two
			commands, with the comparison rows. The example also generates its sample sheet with the command it
			then uses.
	New - docs/tutorial.md: CAMI2 walkthrough with expected output
		A v2 counterpart of the v1 tutorial. CAMI2 is simulated with a known truth, so output that differs
			from the tutorial's points to the local setup.
		Two parts: a ten-minute smoke test on the `tiny` fixture in the repository, with no download, and a
			full walkthrough on three CAMI2 gastrointestinal samples (read_qc, assembly, binning, bin_refinement,
			quant_bins, classify_bins, then `annotate --kofam` and `pathways`). Each step gives a measured cost
			from the committed results or says the cost is not measured, and the tutorial ends with the clean-HQ
			count to compare against.
	New - MIMAG quality tags on every refined bin set
		`mimag_tag`, `mimag_rrna` and `mimag_trnas` are appended to `.stats.tsv`, following Bowers et al. (Nat
			Biotechnol 2017). Completeness and contamination were already measured, and `annotate --bakta`
			already runs tRNAscan-SE, Aragorn and Infernal, so the rRNA and tRNA evidence is parsed from existing
			output.
		A genome annotated without that evidence is tagged `high_quality_unverified_rrna` with
			`mimag_rrna=unknown`, not downgraded to medium. A bin above 10% contamination is `not_mimag` (no
			MIMAG tier), not low quality.
		Boundaries are Bowers' inequalities (>90, <5, >=50, 18 of 20 tRNAs), which differ from the existing
			`quality_counts` tiers at 90.00 and 5.00; both are pinned. tRNAs are counted as distinct amino acids,
			not as features. `bin_refinement` prints the tier distribution of its final set.
	New - `kraken2 --bracken`: abundance at a chosen rank
		Kraken2 reports the reads assigned at each node. A read matching conserved sequence is assigned at a
			higher rank, so a species at 10% of a community can have only part of its reads at its own node and
			the rest at genus or family. Bracken (Lu et al., PeerJ Comput Sci 2017) redistributes them using the
			Kraken2 database's k-mer distribution. On the test fixture, 150 of 1,000 reads at the species node
			(15%) become 301 estimated reads (31.7%).
		Options are `--bracken-rank` (default species) and `--bracken-read-length`. Bracken needs Kraken2's
			`--report`, which `kraken2` did not write; the flag now adds it. Without `--bracken`, Kraken2's
			output is byte-identical to before.
		Four checks cover cases that otherwise give no error: a rank the database was not built for, a read
			length absent from the database (or shorter than its k), a `-w` report passed instead of the table,
			and a redistribution that moves almost nothing, which means the Bracken and Kraken2 databases do not
			match. Bracken exits 0 on every error, so its output is checked instead of its exit status.
		No extra download is needed: the pre-built KRAKEN2 standard database includes the
			`databaseNmers.kmer_distrib` files for 50 to 300 bp. A custom database needs `bracken-build -d
			$KRAKEN2_DB -l <read length>`, with `BRACKEN_DB` pointing at it.
	Fixed - dbCAN `-|-` EC field read as an EC number
		dbCAN joins a multi-domain gene's per-domain fields with `|` and writes `-` for a domain it cannot
			annotate, so a two-domain gene with no EC number gives `-|-`. The cleaner split only on `;` and `,`,
			so `ec` came back as `-|-`, which any count of EC-annotated genes would include. This affected 2 of
			11 rows in the vendored fixture. The trailing dash of a partial EC number (`3.2.1.-`) is kept,
			because whole members are dropped instead of dashes stripped. Found by the parser tests below.
	Added - parser tests for other tools' tables (about 45, restored)
		The suite stays small: golden tests, one main path per module, and regressions for defects that
			produced wrong output. These parser tests are added back because several past defects came from
			parsers, each giving a plausible wrong number instead of an error: GUNC's `pass.GUNC` as `1.0`/`0.0`
			(a 166-bin set scored 0% non-chimeric), inStrain's identities as fractions where its paper says
			percentages (42 of 42 comparisons read as different strains), SingleM's read fraction already being a
			percentage (0.8% read as 80%), the depth table's interleaved `-var` columns, and `bin.13` collapsing
			to `bin` under splitext.
		Six files cover GUNC, CheckM1 and CheckM2, the depth table, KOfam's ko_list, Pfam, dbCAN, GECCO,
			BRITE's htext, SingleM, nonpareil, PPanGGOLiN, IQ-TREE, inStrain, TRACS, AMRFinderPlus, RGI and
			argNorm. Each pins the column names the tool writes, the unit, and identifier handling. A missing
			value must come back as None or blank, never as zero.
	New - `assembly --keep-graph`, and `run`/`cohort` record which strategy built a catalogue
		Plasmid-aware binning (PlasMAAG), graph-aware binners (GraphMB) and Hi-C placement read the assembly
			graph, which meant re-running assembly. `--keep-graph` puts the graph at a named path. metaSPAdes'
			`assembly_graph_with_scaffolds.gfa` and metaFlye's `assembly_graph.gfa` are hard-linked (121 MB and
			2.3 MB, no extra disk). MEGAHIT writes no graph, so one is built from its intermediates with
			`megahit_toolkit contig2fastg` (235 MB, two seconds on one core), from the largest k it wrote, which
			can differ from the configured k. Off by default and a separate resumable step, so `--resume
			--keep-graph` adds a graph to a finished assembly.
		Correction to the earlier plans: `assembly` never deleted a graph. metaSPAdes and metaFlye graphs were
			kept, and MEGAHIT does not write one.
		The study manifest now records `strategy` (co-assembly or per-sample), `samples` and `dereplicated`,
			and `status` prints them. Previously nothing recorded which kind of catalogue a directory held; the
			two have different genome counts and redundancy and are not comparable.
	New - `metawrap2 report`: PDF report and per-genome table
		A finished study wrote twenty-odd tables, none of them a document to share and none per genome
			(quality, taxonomy and coverage were in separate files). `report` reads the module output directories
			it is given and assembles what it finds: a summary page, read QC, assembly statistics, the per-genome
			table with its quality-tier distribution, abundance across samples, the functional wheels, and row
			counts for resistance, viruses and strains. It computes nothing.
		One page lists every module whose output was absent and what was looked for, so a missing output is
			not read as a negative result; the page is included even when nothing is missing. Blank cells are
			measurements that were not made, never zeros. A genome named by only one input (for example an
			abundance table over a different bin set) is listed with the count stated: 13 of 26 rows on the
			reference run.
		`--mag-table` writes the per-genome table beside the PDF, joining the stats table, the GUNC report,
			`classify_bins`' taxonomy and `quant_bins`' abundance on bin name across the three spellings those
			files use (`bin_001`, `bin_001.fasta`, `bin.1`). The PDF uses matplotlib's PdfPages (already a
			dependency), needs no HTML toolchain or headless browser, and renders the same on a cluster with no
			network.
		Two defects were found by running it on a real per-sample study rather than a fixture. It reported one
			sample's bin set as the whole study (`Genomes (24)` for a study that recovered 62) because it took
			the first stats table it found. The GUNC search also joined sample_6's chimerism report onto
			sample_13's genomes, because every sample numbers its bins from `bin_001`, giving 23 wrong verdicts
			and one phantom genome. Each bin set is now paired with the GUNC report in its own directory, rows
			have a `sample` column when there is more than one bin set, and the heading says what it covers.
		Provenance is one line per module on page 1 plus a verbatim appendix (it was six pages before the
			first result). The reference run gives 23 pages, first result on page 2.
	New - `pathways --brite-db`, and a PNG beside every Krona wheel
		The BRITE hierarchy is downloaded rather than vendored, and a revised hierarchy changes the
			percentages. Every other database has both a config key and a flag; BRITE had only `KEGG_BRITE`, so
			selecting a snapshot meant editing metawrap2.toml. The flag overrides the config key, and the skip
			message names all three ways to supply one.
	New - static PNG wheels of the functional hierarchy
		`ktImportText` writes only an interactive HTML file, which cannot go into a report, release note,
			paper figure or message, and rasterising Krona needs a headless browser. `metawrap2.sunburst` draws
			the same data with matplotlib (already a hard dependency) from the same file Krona reads, so the two
			agree. `pathways` writes one PNG beside each Krona HTML.
		BRITE's scaffolding branches are kept, because dropping them inflates every remaining percentage.
			Level A is drawn in KEGG's order with a fixed colour per branch, so wheels from two samples can be
			compared directly. Small wedges are drawn but not labelled, and the caption gives how many pathway
			wedges fall below the label threshold and their total share.
	New - `annotate --eggnog`: GO terms, EC numbers and COG categories from eggNOG-mapper
		eggNOG-mapper (Cantalapiedra et al., Mol Biol Evol 2021) runs over the same proteins as `--kofam`
			and `--pfam`. Annotations are transferred from the orthologous group a gene falls in, not
			matched against a profile. Each row keeps `max_annot_lvl`, the taxonomic level of that group.
		Five development bins, 14,171 genes, 1,134 s wall for all three searches at -t 24 (6.5 GB peak,
			13.1 cores busy; eggNOG about 100 s per bin, nearly all DIAMOND): --kofam placed 5,827 genes
			(41.1%), --pfam 11,369 (80.2%), --eggnog 13,443 (94.9%), and 12,634 (89.2%) got a GO term, EC
			number or COG category, 41,075 terms in all. No module produced GO terms or COG categories
			before this; EC numbers came only from `--cazy`.
		eggNOG's KOs are written beside KOfam's, not merged: 5,077 genes called by both (4,841 with the
			same KO), 2,295 by eggNOG only, 750 by KOfam only. The overlap is printed.
		`install-db eggnog` downloads 12 GB and unpacks to 48 GB (eggnog.db 39 GB, eggnog_proteins.dmnd
			8.7 GB), so it is opt-in and its plan lists every URL. eggNOG-mapper 2.1.15 imports
			`distutils`, removed in Python 3.12, so it has its own environment (`envs/eggnog.yaml`, Python
			below 3.12).
		Two defects found while running it: the first installer pointed at a host that no longer serves
			the files and reported `Finished` after three 0-byte downloads (it now uses eggnog5.embl.de
			through the shared checked download path), and `EGGNOG_DB` named `eggnog.db` instead of the
			directory, the pattern that lost Pfam's release above.
	New - metaWRAP 1 comparison on CAMI2 oral and skin
		All defaults were measured on three CAMI2 gastrointestinal samples. The comparison now also runs on
			oral and skin with the same design: three samples, per-sample assembly, metaWRAP 1.3.0 beside the
			current default, both scored with CheckM2 and GUNC. Skin is a low-diversity community
			dominated by a few genera.
		Clean high-quality MAGs, counted per bin by joining the two reports:
			  body site          metaWRAP 1.3.0   MetaWrap2 default   cpu-h (mw1 vs mw2)
			  gastrointestinal   48               53  (+10%)          132.0 vs 127.1
			  oral               45               51  (+13%)          172.6 vs 184.9
			  skin               41               50  (+22%)          136.2 vs 200.2
		MetaWrap2 is ahead on all three sites, most on skin. Per sample it is ahead on five of the six oral
			and skin samples and level on one (oral 20/17, 13/10, 18/18; skin 14/10, 17/16, 19/15).
		The gastrointestinal arms were re-scored with the per-bin join instead of reusing their older
			high-quality counts, so all six numbers use the same method. Cost is not comparable across sites:
			the gastrointestinal arm trained SemiBin2 on a GPU (0.6 GPU-hours) and the oral and skin arms
			ran the CPU default, which accounts for the 124 and 139 vs 32 cpu-hour binning difference.
			Within a site, MetaWrap2 is 4% cheaper on gastrointestinal, 7% more expensive on oral and 47%
			more expensive on skin, where it recovers 9 more clean MAGs for 64 more cpu-hours.
		Figure: docs/figures/body_sites.png.
	New - binning presets measured on CAMI2 oral
		The preset table was from the gastrointestinal samples only. All presets now have oral numbers,
			scored with the same per-bin CheckM2 and GUNC join:
			  arm                     binners                        device  clean  HQ   cpu-h
			  --preset fast           metaBAT2 + MaxBin2             cpu      39    50     9.4
			  metaWRAP 1.3.0          metaBAT2 + MaxBin2 + CONCOCT   cpu      45    49   123.4
			  --preset default        metaBAT2 + SemiBin2            cpu      51    57   132.8
			  --preset slow           + COMEBin                      cuda     59    63    80.3
		`slow` recovers 16% more clean MAGs than `default` on oral (13% on gastrointestinal) and is ahead
			on each sample (23/20, 15/13, 21/18). A user-testing run on MetaHIT reads gave +58%.
		The cost column is binning plus refinement, since the preset arms reuse the default arm's
			assemblies. `default` trained SemiBin2 on CPU and `slow` used a GPU, so `slow` shows fewer
			CPU-hours. The comparable pair is fast and default, both on CPU: 9.4 against 132.8 cpu-hours
			for 39 against 51 clean MAGs.
		No default changed. Making `slow` the default needs its cost measured with SemiBin2 and COMEBin on
			CPU, which has not been done.
		Figure: docs/figures/oral_tiers.png.
	New - binning presets measured on CAMI2 skin
		The same four arms on the three skin samples, scored with the same per-bin CheckM2 and GUNC join:
			  arm                     binners                        device  clean  HQ   cpu-h
			  --preset fast           metaBAT2 + MaxBin2             cpu      35    41    10.7
			  metaWRAP 1.3.0          metaBAT2 + MaxBin2 + CONCOCT   cpu      41    48    81.0
			  --preset default        metaBAT2 + SemiBin2            cpu      50    57   147.7
			  --preset slow           + COMEBin                      cuda     54    59   122.3
		`slow` recovers 8% more clean MAGs than `default` on skin (16% on oral, 13% on
			gastrointestinal) and wins two samples and ties one (15/14, 17/17, 22/19). `fast` recovers
			fewer clean MAGs than metaWRAP 1 (35 against 41) for 13% of its compute.
		On sample_17 CheckM1 needed `--reduced_tree` for COMEBin's bin set, so that sample's hybrid-set
			choice used a slightly different scorer; the reported counts come from the shared join.
		No default changed. The three sites agree on the direction of `slow`'s gain but not its size,
			so it stays opt-in.
		Figures: docs/figures/body_sites.png, docs/figures/skin_tiers.png.
	New - cami2_oral and cami2_skin benchmark datasets
		The CAMI2 deposit (frl:6425518, CC BY 4.0, Meyer et al. 2022) has 49 short-read samples across five
			body sites with the same truth format. `cami2_oral` and `cami2_skin` each take three samples,
			matching the three-sample design of the metaWRAP 1 comparison.
		md5s are pinned from the publisher's manifest, and every byte count is measured with a HEAD request,
			as benchmarks/datasets.toml requires. Body sites were checked after extraction, not taken from
			sample numbering. Oral: 88 of 89, 176 of 177 and 159 of 159 of each sample's genomes are in the
			oral gold standard, against 4-5 in the gastrointestinal one.
		The deposit's 29 airskinurogenital samples are not labelled by site. 88 of 90, 50 of 52 and 238 of
			240 of the genomes in samples 17, 18 and 19 are in the skin gold standard, about half that in the
			airways one and almost none in the urogenital one. The sites are interleaved in the deposit;
			the full per-sample assignment is in benchmarks/datasets.toml.
MetaWrap2 v=2.1.2`, and the README asks for this
			string in bug reports. It now prints `MetaWrap2 2.1.3 (git acbd3d1)` from a git checkout and the
			plain version from a wheel. The git call has a 2 s timeout and a silent fallback, so it cannot fail
			or hang.
	Fixed - README example step 3 used raw reads
		Step 3 of the three-command example used the raw `reads_1.fastq reads_2.fastq` after step 2 had used
			`READ_QC/final_pure_reads_*`, and dropped the inputs' `.gz`. As written, it either fails on a missing
			file or bins reads that were not QC'd or host-filtered. The positional arguments also sat next to a
			flag that takes a value, so it was unclear whether `--preset` was taking a path.
	Changed - upper bounds on pandas and numpy
		`pandas>=1.3` and `numpy>=1.20` resolved a fresh install to pandas 3.0.6 and numpy 2.5.4, two major
			versions with breaking changes that MetaWrap2 has not been run against. Upper bounds now state the
			tested range.
	Changed - Python 3.10+ and conda are stated in the install block
		The requirement was in `## Notes`, 250 lines below the install block. On stock RHEL 8 or older Ubuntu,
			`python3` is 3.9, and `pip install .` fails after downloading scipy, pyrodigal and seaborn, with
			nothing saying where to get a 3.10+ interpreter. The install block now gives the requirement, the
			Miniforge bootstrap, and whether conda is needed first.
		The sentence "`cohort` is `run --no-coassembly`" moved from usage.md to the README beside the two
			commands, with the comparison rows. The example also generates its sample sheet with the command it
			then uses.
	New - docs/tutorial.md: CAMI2 walkthrough with expected output
		A v2 counterpart of the v1 tutorial. CAMI2 is simulated with a known truth, so output that differs
			from the tutorial's points to the local setup.
		Two parts: a ten-minute smoke test on the `tiny` fixture in the repository, with no download, and a
			full walkthrough on three CAMI2 gastrointestinal samples (read_qc, assembly, binning, bin_refinement,
			quant_bins, classify_bins, then `annotate --kofam` and `pathways`). Each step gives a measured cost
			from the committed results or says the cost is not measured, and the tutorial ends with the clean-HQ
			count to compare against.
	New - MIMAG quality tags on every refined bin set
		`mimag_tag`, `mimag_rrna` and `mimag_trnas` are appended to `.stats.tsv`, following Bowers et al. (Nat
			Biotechnol 2017). Completeness and contamination were already measured, and `annotate --bakta`
			already runs tRNAscan-SE, Aragorn and Infernal, so the rRNA and tRNA evidence is parsed from existing
			output.
		A genome annotated without that evidence is tagged `high_quality_unverified_rrna` with
			`mimag_rrna=unknown`, not downgraded to medium. A bin above 10% contamination is `not_mimag` (no
			MIMAG tier), not low quality.
		Boundaries are Bowers' inequalities (>90, <5, >=50, 18 of 20 tRNAs), which differ from the existing
			`quality_counts` tiers at 90.00 and 5.00; both are pinned. tRNAs are counted as distinct amino acids,
			not as features. `bin_refinement` prints the tier distribution of its final set.
	New - `kraken2 --bracken`: abundance at a chosen rank
		Kraken2 reports the reads assigned at each node. A read matching conserved sequence is assigned at a
			higher rank, so a species at 10% of a community can have only part of its reads at its own node and
			the rest at genus or family. Bracken (Lu et al., PeerJ Comput Sci 2017) redistributes them using the
			Kraken2 database's k-mer distribution. On the test fixture, 150 of 1,000 reads at the species node
			(15%) become 301 estimated reads (31.7%).
		Options are `--bracken-rank` (default species) and `--bracken-read-length`. Bracken needs Kraken2's
			`--report`, which `kraken2` did not write; the flag now adds it. Without `--bracken`, Kraken2's
			output is byte-identical to before.
		Four checks cover cases that otherwise give no error: a rank the database was not built for, a read
			length absent from the database (or shorter than its k), a `-w` report passed instead of the table,
			and a redistribution that moves almost nothing, which means the Bracken and Kraken2 databases do not
			match. Bracken exits 0 on every error, so its output is checked instead of its exit status.
		No extra download is needed: the pre-built KRAKEN2 standard database includes the
			`databaseNmers.kmer_distrib` files for 50 to 300 bp. A custom database needs `bracken-build -d
			$KRAKEN2_DB -l <read length>`, with `BRACKEN_DB` pointing at it.
	Fixed - dbCAN `-|-` EC field read as an EC number
		dbCAN joins a multi-domain gene's per-domain fields with `|` and writes `-` for a domain it cannot
			annotate, so a two-domain gene with no EC number gives `-|-`. The cleaner split only on `;` and `,`,
			so `ec` came back as `-|-`, which any count of EC-annotated genes would include. This affected 2 of
			11 rows in the vendored fixture. The trailing dash of a partial EC number (`3.2.1.-`) is kept,
			because whole members are dropped instead of dashes stripped. Found by the parser tests below.
	Added - parser tests for other tools' tables (about 45, restored)
		The suite stays small: golden tests, one main path per module, and regressions for defects that
			produced wrong output. These parser tests are added back because several past defects came from
			parsers, each giving a plausible wrong number instead of an error: GUNC's `pass.GUNC` as `1.0`/`0.0`
			(a 166-bin set scored 0% non-chimeric), inStrain's identities as fractions where its paper says
			percentages (42 of 42 comparisons read as different strains), SingleM's read fraction already being a
			percentage (0.8% read as 80%), the depth table's interleaved `-var` columns, and `bin.13` collapsing
			to `bin` under splitext.
		Six files cover GUNC, CheckM1 and CheckM2, the depth table, KOfam's ko_list, Pfam, dbCAN, GECCO,
			BRITE's htext, SingleM, nonpareil, PPanGGOLiN, IQ-TREE, inStrain, TRACS, AMRFinderPlus, RGI and
			argNorm. Each pins the column names the tool writes, the unit, and identifier handling. A missing
			value must come back as None or blank, never as zero.
	New - `assembly --keep-graph`, and `run`/`cohort` record which strategy built a catalogue
		Plasmid-aware binning (PlasMAAG), graph-aware binners (GraphMB) and Hi-C placement read the assembly
			graph, which meant re-running assembly. `--keep-graph` puts the graph at a named path. metaSPAdes'
			`assembly_graph_with_scaffolds.gfa` and metaFlye's `assembly_graph.gfa` are hard-linked (121 MB and
			2.3 MB, no extra disk). MEGAHIT writes no graph, so one is built from its intermediates with
			`megahit_toolkit contig2fastg` (235 MB, two seconds on one core), from the largest k it wrote, which
			can differ from the configured k. Off by default and a separate resumable step, so `--resume
			--keep-graph` adds a graph to a finished assembly.
		Correction to the earlier plans: `assembly` never deleted a graph. metaSPAdes and metaFlye graphs were
			kept, and MEGAHIT does not write one.
		The study manifest now records `strategy` (co-assembly or per-sample), `samples` and `dereplicated`,
			and `status` prints them. Previously nothing recorded which kind of catalogue a directory held; the
			two have different genome counts and redundancy and are not comparable.
	New - `metawrap2 report`: PDF report and per-genome table
		A finished study wrote twenty-odd tables, none of them a document to share and none per genome
			(quality, taxonomy and coverage were in separate files). `report` reads the module output directories
			it is given and assembles what it finds: a summary page, read QC, assembly statistics, the per-genome
			table with its quality-tier distribution, abundance across samples, the functional wheels, and row
			counts for resistance, viruses and strains. It computes nothing.
		One page lists every module whose output was absent and what was looked for, so a missing output is
			not read as a negative result; the page is included even when nothing is missing. Blank cells are
			measurements that were not made, never zeros. A genome named by only one input (for example an
			abundance table over a different bin set) is listed with the count stated: 13 of 26 rows on the
			reference run.
		`--mag-table` writes the per-genome table beside the PDF, joining the stats table, the GUNC report,
			`classify_bins`' taxonomy and `quant_bins`' abundance on bin name across the three spellings those
			files use (`bin_001`, `bin_001.fasta`, `bin.1`). The PDF uses matplotlib's PdfPages (already a
			dependency), needs no HTML toolchain or headless browser, and renders the same on a cluster with no
			network.
		Two defects were found by running it on a real per-sample study rather than a fixture. It reported one
			sample's bin set as the whole study (`Genomes (24)` for a study that recovered 62) because it took
			the first stats table it found. The GUNC search also joined sample_6's chimerism report onto
			sample_13's genomes, because every sample numbers its bins from `bin_001`, giving 23 wrong verdicts
			and one phantom genome. Each bin set is now paired with the GUNC report in its own directory, rows
			have a `sample` column when there is more than one bin set, and the heading says what it covers.
		Provenance is one line per module on page 1 plus a verbatim appendix (it was six pages before the
			first result). The reference run gives 23 pages, first result on page 2.
	New - `pathways --brite-db`, and a PNG beside every Krona wheel
		The BRITE hierarchy is downloaded rather than vendored, and a revised hierarchy changes the
			percentages. Every other database has both a config key and a flag; BRITE had only `KEGG_BRITE`, so
			selecting a snapshot meant editing metawrap2.toml. The flag overrides the config key, and the skip
			message names all three ways to supply one.
	New - static PNG wheels of the functional hierarchy
		`ktImportText` writes only an interactive HTML file, which cannot go into a report, release note,
			paper figure or message, and rasterising Krona needs a headless browser. `metawrap2.sunburst` draws
			the same data with matplotlib (already a hard dependency) from the same file Krona reads, so the two
			agree. `pathways` writes one PNG beside each Krona HTML.
		BRITE's scaffolding branches are kept, because dropping them inflates every remaining percentage.
			Level A is drawn in KEGG's order with a fixed colour per branch, so wheels from two samples can be
			compared directly. Small wedges are drawn but not labelled, and the caption gives how many pathway
			wedges fall below the label threshold and their total share.
	New - metaWRAP 1 comparison on CAMI2 oral
		All defaults were measured on three CAMI2 gastrointestinal samples. The comparison now also runs on
			oral with the same design: three samples, per-sample assembly, metaWRAP 1.3.0 beside the current
			default, both scored with CheckM2 and GUNC.
		Clean high-quality MAGs, counted per bin by joining the two reports:
			  body site          metaWRAP 1.3.0   MetaWrap2 default   cpu-h (mw1 vs mw2)
			  gastrointestinal   48               53  (+10%)          132.0 vs 127.1
			  oral               45               51  (+13%)          172.6 vs 184.9
		The margin is slightly larger on oral. Per sample on oral, MetaWrap2 is ahead on two and level on the
			third (20/17, 13/10, 18/18).
		The gastrointestinal arms were re-scored with the per-bin join instead of reusing their older
			high-quality counts, so all four numbers use the same method. Cost is not comparable across sites:
			the gastrointestinal arm trained SemiBin2 on a GPU (0.6 GPU-hours) and the oral arm ran the CPU
			default, which accounts for all of the 124 vs 32 cpu-hour binning difference. Within a site,
			MetaWrap2 is 4% cheaper on gastrointestinal and 7% more expensive on oral.
		Figure: docs/figures/body_sites.png.
	New - cami2_oral benchmark dataset
		The CAMI2 deposit (frl:6425518, CC BY 4.0, Meyer et al. 2022) has 49 short-read samples across five
			body sites with the same truth format. `cami2_oral` takes three of the oral samples, matching the
			three-sample design of the metaWRAP 1 comparison.
		md5s are pinned from the publisher's manifest, and every byte count is measured with a HEAD request,
			as benchmarks/datasets.toml requires. The body site was checked after extraction, not taken from
			sample numbering: 88 of 89, 176 of 177 and 159 of 159 of each sample's genomes are in the oral gold
			standard, against 4-5 in the gastrointestinal one.
MetaWrap2 v=2.1.2
	Functional annotation (gene calling over bins or an assembly, KEGG orthologs at curated thresholds,
		module completeness, functional hierarchy and Krona wheels), AMR with context and an evidence
		level, viruses, strains, community appraisal, long reads and comparative genomics.
	New - `metawrap2 annotate`, replacing the Prokka wrapper `annotate_bins`: Pyrodigal gene calls over a
		bin folder or a whole assembly, one table every later step reads. Bins under 20 kb fall back to
		meta mode with a notice. Each gene carries `partial` and `distance_to_contig_end`.
	New - `annotate --kofam`: KO assignment at KOfam's per-profile cutoffs, compared on the score type each
		profile names (17,890 `full`, 8,697 `domain`). The 1,866 of 28,453 thresholdless profiles are dropped
		by default; `--kofam-relative-fraction` adds a fallback tagged evidence=relative (0.5x: +355 to 949).
	New - `annotate --kofam` accepts KOfam's per-KO profile directory (27,930 files) and searches it in one
		batch: 2,339 genes in 80 seconds at >1000% CPU (was one single-core search per file, >10 min).
	New - `annotate --pfam`: Pfam-A domains at each family's gathering threshold, per domain, with
		`pfam_architectures.tsv` and versioned accessions. One bin: KEGG places 1,620 of 3,891 genes (41.6%),
		Pfam 3,185 (81.9%), 1,578 with no KO; only 13 have a KO and no Pfam domain. InterProScan not wrapped.
	New - `annotate --cazy`: dbCAN in `--mode protein` on `annotate`'s proteins, with method agreement per
		row (two CAMI2 bins: 415 CAZyme genes, 274 backed by >=2 methods). CBM is kept apart from the
		catalytic classes. `metawrap2 install-db dbcan` fetches the pinned 7.4 GB release via `--aws_s3`.
	New - `metawrap2 pathways`: KEGG module completeness, both `pathwise` and `stepwise`. A `+`-joined
		complex counts as one step (31 of 573 modules are a single complex). Per-row flags: step count (47
		single-step), signature module, reactions with no KO (14), KO promiscuity, definition snapshot.
	New - functional hierarchy (BRITE ko00001: 8 branches, 57 processes, 570 pathways, 28,545 genes) per
		genome and per sample (abundance-weighted), with Krona wheels. A KO's weight is split across its
		pathways. The two non-biological branches are flagged; full and pathway-only wheels are written.
	New - `pathways -C depth.txt` (contig depth) and `pathways -G gene_abundance.tsv` (from `catalogue
		--quantify`) weight the sample hierarchy without MAGs. Gene-weighted profile: 33% "Not Included in
		Pathway or Brite" vs ~14% MAG-weighted. Every hierarchy row has a `weighting` column; one file each.
	New - `metawrap2 amr`: AMRFinderPlus on nucleotides over bins or an assembly, before any length filter.
		Each row has distance to contig end, a three-state context (`context_unknown` when not searched) and
		a Cao et al. 2026 evidence level (L1/L2 only). Virulence in its own table; co-located genes clustered.
	New - `amr --mobile-elements DIR` reads a `viruses` or geNomad output for MGE context. One CAMI2
		assembly, 18 calls: 5 on a plasmid contig, 1 with nothing within 10 kb, 12 undetermined. Dev bins:
		all four calls move from `context_unknown`/L1 to `no_mge_in_window`/L2.
	New - `amr --rgi`: RGI against CARD in its own env (`metawrap2 install-db card`, 4 MB). Joined with
		AMRFinderPlus on ARO accessions: five CAMI2 bins give 4 and 8 calls, 2 in common. RGI's tier
		(Perfect/Strict, Nudged suffix) is in `method`; `amr.tsv` gains an `aro` column.
	New - `amr` writes `amr_hamronized.tsv` (hAMRonization, 36 fields, tool and database versions read
		inside the env; `--skip-hamronize` turns it off) and `amr_normalised.tsv` (argNorm: ARO and drugs).
		argNorm gets only the AMRFinderPlus rows. Unmapped calls are kept and counted.
	New - `metawrap2 viruses`: geNomad and CheckV, the viral counterpart of binning plus refinement. One
		CAMI2 assembly: 0 viral contigs in the bins, 161 >=2 kb in the assembly (4 MIUViG high-quality), 344
		plasmids. CheckV's tiers; 16 unscored contigs are `undetermined`; 51 are lower bounds, so flagged.
	New - `viruses` clusters vOTUs at 95% ANI over 85% of the shorter sequence, longest member as
		representative (`--skip-votus` to skip). 161 contigs gave 161 vOTUs; a duplicate control collapsed.
	New - `viruses --crispr-hosts BIN_FOLDER`: CCTyper spacers matched exactly against viral contigs, trusted
		arrays only (`--untrusted-arrays` to include the rest). Dev set: 54 spacers, 0 matches to 161 viral
		contigs; the positive control finds all 54 in their own genomes.
	New - `metawrap2 appraise` (SingleM; `metawrap2 install-db singlem`): community share in the bins.
		ERR011347's 26 bins: 11.6% of bacteria, 25% of archaea; 323 lineages have no genome. Needs forward
		reads only, >=60 bp (34 bp trimmed reads gave 10 marker sequences, raw 75 bp reads 1,431).
	New - `metawrap2 nonpareil`: read-based coverage per sample and a cohort verdict. Three gut samples:
		84.4%, 74.6%, 89.4% coverage; Nd 17.97, 18.58, 17.27; 4.1x, 8.0x, 2.5x more sequencing to reach 95%.
		Model fit ported to Python, matches the R package to six significant figures.
	New - `nonpareil --kernel auto` picks one kernel per cohort: k-mer, or alignment if any sample's mean
		read length is under 2k. The kernels disagree (50.5% vs 83.0% on one sample). Reads shorter than k are
		filtered and reported; the alignment kernel is seeded `-r 1337`, the k-mer kernel cannot be.
	New - `metawrap2 strains` (inStrain): popANI, diversity gated on `breadth_minCov`, `--bam` for existing
		alignments. ERR011347-9 vs 26 bins: 42 comparisons, 17 at popANI >=99.999%, 4 over enough genome
		(60-92%) to count as shared strains.
	New - `strains --backend {instrain,tracs,both}`: TRACS transmission distances beside inStrain, never
		averaged. Both call bin.1 and bin.4 shared between ERR011347 and ERR011348. TRACS needs 25% breadth.
	New - `metawrap2 catalogue`: non-redundant gene catalogue across samples with MMseqs2 linclust (95%
		identity, 90% member coverage, `--cov-mode 1 --cluster-mode 2`). Gene ids are prefixed
		`<source>~<gene_id>`; `members.tsv` keeps the originals. No new install.
	New - `catalogue --quantify`: salmon (`--no-clip`) maps reads to the catalogue, writes gene abundance
		and a per-sample mapping rate, warns below 50%. Controls: own reads 100.0%, unrelated reads 0.68%.
	New - `metawrap2 quantify_function`: per-genome features weighted by genome abundance into sample tables
		in three units; the cross-sample unit needs `--microbial-fraction`. Module presence is binary above a
		threshold. `--abundance-kind` says whether to divide by genome size. Rows carry genome counts.
	New - `metawrap2 diff_abundance`: ANCOM-BC (scikit-bio 0.7.1), `feature ~ group` only. Refuses fewer
		than three samples or one group. Prevalence filter off by default; all settings recorded.
	New - `--biom` writes BIOM 2.1 (HDF5) tables, with the unit stored as sample metadata.
	New - long reads: `assembly -L reads.fastq --read-type ont-hq` (metaFlye) and `binning --long-read-type`
		(minimap2 preset, SemiBin2 long-read mode). `long_read_report.tsv` checks the declared platform.
		Simulated 25x R10 reads: 72 contigs, 2.41 Mbp, N50 57.9 kb, 4 bins.
	New - `metawrap2 pangenome` (PPanGGOLiN over dereplication clusters) and `metawrap2 phylogeny` (IQ-TREE
		on GTDB-Tk or `pangenome --msa` alignments, occupancy beside support). Cohort: 46 clusters gave 7
		pangenomes; <15 genomes is `partitioning_reliable=no`; <50% persistent is flagged.
	New - `binning --binsplit SEP`: multi-split binning of a prefixed catalogue, split per sample into
		`<binner>_bins_split/` and flat `<binner>_split_bins/`. Not a default: 29 clean HQ MAGs vs the cohort
		path's 34, 9.09 vs 8.71 cpu-hours, GUNC pass 70.2% vs 87.0%. Söding's +19%/+79% did not reproduce.
	New - bin_refinement reports the binned fraction in base pairs (CAMI2: 17.8% of contigs, 58.7% of bp)
		and a chimerism caveat (54.5-81.8% of HQ MAGs are single-strain; Treichel et al. 2026).
	New - measured module completeness vs MAG completeness (one CAMI2 genome): complete modules at 100/95/
		90/80/70/50% = 58/52.7/48.0/37.0/24.0/15.0. At 70/10, 59% are gone. `pathways` rows carry CheckM2
		completeness. Draws at 80% gave 31, 39 and 41.
	New - vendored, dated KEGG snapshots: modules_table.tsv (573 modules; live KEGG has 582), ko_list.gz
		(28,453 profiles), ko_hierarchy.tsv.gz (64,956 KO-pathway pairs). KOfam HMMs are not vendored (KOFAM_DB).
	New - docs: `## Function` and `## Viruses` in docs/modules.md, a worked chain in docs/usage.md
		(annotate -> pathways -> quantify_function -> amr -> diff_abundance). README updated to the current modules.
	Changed - CoverM in quant_bins filters reads at 90% identity and 75% aligned (were CoverM's 0 and 0).
		No flag restores 0/0.
	Changed - pins: `gunc >=1.1.1` (was >=1.0.6), `checkm2 >=1.1.0` (was >=1.0.2; also fixes the
		`_pywrap_checkpoint_reader` SystemError), `gtdbtk <2.5.0` (2.7.x wants R232 and ~140 GB RAM).
	Changed - `install-env --all` now also builds checkm2, gtdbtk, benchmark-amber and learned. It skipped
		them before, including SemiBin2, which the default binning tier needs.
	Changed - 2.0.x per-tool env names (nine) resolve to the 2.1 workflow env with a notice. Unknown names are
		refused with a did-you-mean. install-env, doctor and check share one resolver: 38 names over 11 envs.
	Changed - the MEGABLAST + taxator-tk path in `classify_bins` is deprecated (no upstream release since
		2015) and warns once. Default unchanged. `--gtdbtk` needs ~110 GB of GTDB data; nt needs hundreds of GB.
	Changed - `annotate_bins` is deprecated in favour of `annotate`; it still works.
	Changed - QUALITY_TIERS comments: `bins_medium_quality` (>=50/<10) is MIMAG medium, `bins_near_complete`
		(>=90/<5) is MIMAG HQ without rRNA/tRNA, `bins_high_quality` (>=70/<10) is not a MIMAG tier.
		Field names unchanged.
	Fixed - install-env on 2.0.1 could not reach bakta, checkm2 or gtdbtk under any name (KeyError, "No env
		file"). doctor used env names that did not exist, and check printed "Preflight OK" after checking
		nothing.
	Fixed - `check` looked for tools on PATH; it now looks inside the module's conda env (PATH only with
		`use_conda_envs = false`). A tool missing from an existing env is reported as half-built (`--force`).
	Fixed - four help strings named envs removed in 2.1.0 (`metawrap2-annotate_bins-bakta`,
		`metawrap2-classify_bins-gtdbtk` twice, `metawrap2-bin_refinement-dastool`); a test now scans for these.
	Fixed - GUNC's pass column written as `1.0`/`0.0` was read as failing, in bin_refinement and the
		scorer: one 166-bin set scored 0 pass, wrong by 92 bins. `nan` is now unassessed, not failed;
		`gunc_unassessed_fraction` is reported.
	Fixed - `benchmark run --max-samples` kept one mate of each paired sample, and deinterleaved all ten CAMI2
		samples first (190 GB written for 57 GB used). Interleaved sample labels are left alone.
	Fixed - cctyper 1.9.0 from bioconda could not find its database (MetaWrap2 passes `--db`) and crashed
		on pandas 3 (envs/viruses.yaml pins `pandas <3`).
	Fixed - `install-env --from-lock` never used the lockfiles: the option was parsed and ignored, so every
		install solved the yaml. It now installs `envs/locks/<env>.<platform>.lock`, and says
		so when an environment has no lockfile for the platform and falls back to solving.
	Fixed - `install-env --lock binning` wrote `binning.linux-64.lock`; lockfiles are now named after the
		environment (`bins.linux-64.lock`), which is the name `--from-lock` reads.
	Fixed - `metawrap2 --dry-run install-env` built the environments instead of printing the commands.
	Fixed - pyrodigal and pyhmmer were imported but not declared, so `annotate` failed after a plain `pip
		install`. Both are now dependencies (pyproject and the conda recipe); a test checks that every
		module-level import is declared in both.
	Fixed - the top-level help ran `quantify_function` into its description; the column now fits the
		longest name. The `binning` entry lists all six binners.
	Changed - Python 3.14 is supported and tested in CI (3.10 to 3.14).
	Changed - the test suite is cut from about 1,360 tests to about 180: the golden tests, one main-path test
		per module, and the regression tests for defects that produced wrong output or lost runs. CI's
		CLI smoke test now reads the module list from the CLI, so new modules are covered.
	Changed - KOfam's thresholds and the BRITE hierarchy are downloaded at install time rather than shipped:
		`install-db kofam` (profiles plus ko_list) and `install-db kegg` (BRITE ko00001, config key
		KEGG_BRITE). Rows record the threshold list's md5 and the hierarchy's download date; `annotate`
		warns on a threshold list other than the validated one. `pathways` without KEGG_BRITE writes
		module completeness only.
	Changed - the Lockfiles workflow re-solves every environment monthly, one runner per environment, and
		pushes changed lockfiles to `chore/refresh-lockfiles`. It fails only on a broken solve or install,
		no longer when the repository does not let Actions open pull requests.
	Known issue - amr, viruses, strains, comparative, singlem, rgi, tracs and benchmark-amber have no
		lockfile yet, so `--from-lock` solves them from the yaml. The next Lockfiles run generates them.
	Known issue - geNomad needs the numpy pin in envs/viruses.yaml. A numpy in ~/.local can still shadow it
		under a bare `mamba run`; MetaWrap2 sets PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 for its own env-wrapped commands.
	Known issue - long-read assemblies are not polished with short reads or merged with short-read
		assemblies.
MetaWrap2 v=2.1.1
	Ensemble `slow` binning tier, tier costs read from run logs, and plans for functional annotation.
	Changed - `--preset slow` runs metaBAT2 + SemiBin2 + COMEBin (was COMEBin alone): end to end 64 HQ /
		60 clean vs 61 / 57, for 120.7 vs 80.8 cpu-hours and 2.4 vs 1.7 GPU-hours. `--comebin` restores the
		old tier. On the gold assembly the two tie (177 vs 176 clean).
	Changed - metaBAT2 is the third member on cost: a MaxBin2 trio covers 182 genomes vs 181, but MaxBin2
		costs 24.5 cpu-hours vs ~1.5 and 37.7% of its bins pass GUNC.
	New - a tier's cpu-hours and GPU-hours are read from `mw2 run` logs. The `default` tier's GPU figure is
		now 0.6 (was 0.8). Re-run counts matched: 14/14, 20/19, 30/27.
	New - docs/plan_functional_annotation.md and docs/plan_other_modules.md (not implemented). Licence
		findings: CARD/RGI and geNomad are non-commercial; KOfam's HMM profiles carry no licence statement.
MetaWrap2 v=2.1.0
	Measured defaults (binning tier, aligner, depth identity floor, trimmer, quantifier), learned binners,
		cohorts, GUNC chimerism checks and the benchmark harness.
	Changed - `run` bins with metaBAT2 + SemiBin2 (`--preset default`); was metaBAT2 + MaxBin2 + CONCOCT.
		End to end: 53 clean HQ MAGs vs metaWRAP 1's 48, 43.8 vs 52.5 cpu-hours. Needs metawrap2-learned
		(~1.5 GB pytorch). `--preset fast` gives metaBAT2 + MaxBin2; `--metabat2 --maxbin2 --concoct` the old set.
	New - `binning --preset {very-fast,fast,default,slow}`. End to end on per-sample assemblies:
		  tier              HQ   clean   cpu-h
		  very-fast         39     36     92.1
		  fast              45     41     97.1
		  metaWRAP 1.3.0    52     48    132.0
		  default           56     53    127.1
		  slow              61     57    164.1
		Gold assembly, clean MAGs: very-fast 35, fast 59, default 114, slow 176. In this release slow is
		COMEBin alone (see 2.1.1). Named binners override a preset.
	Changed - the depth-table identity floor is 90% (was jgi's 97%). CAMI2: 47 clean HQ MAGs vs 35, AMBER
		F1 0.6624 vs 0.6371. `binning --min-identity 97` / `coverage --min-identity 97` restore it.
	Changed - binning maps with minimap2 (was bwa): depth Pearson 0.9461, AMBER F1 0.4519 vs 0.4493, 1.368 vs
		7.774 cpu-hours. `--aligner bwa` restores it; `--aligner bwa-mem2` matches bwa at 23.6 GB RAM.
	Changed - read_qc trims with fastp (was Trim Galore): +0.03% reads, +0.83% bases, 5.8% fewer cpu-hours,
		29% less wall time. `--trimmer trim-galore` restores it (0.1 GB peak memory vs fastp's 1.3 GB).
	Changed - quant_bins defaults to sylph (was salmon): Spearman 0.868 vs 0.827, 0.19 vs 1.08 cpu-hours.
		salmon is better on magnitude (Pearson 0.959 vs 0.811); `--quantifier salmon` restores it.
	Changed - `quant_bins --min-ani` defaults to 99 (sylph's own default is 95, where absent genomes took 55%
		of a sample's abundance vs 25%). `--min-ani 95` restores sylph's value; below 95 warns.
	Changed - metaBAT2 is seeded at 1337 (was a random seed per run); CONCOCT's seed 1 is passed explicitly.
		Unseeded runs differed by up to 294 contigs.
	Changed - GUNC runs on the final bins by default and adds gunc_css/gunc_pass to .stats.tsv; bins are
		annotated, never dropped. `--skip-gunc` turns it off (6.7 GB database, `metawrap2 install-db gunc`).
	Changed - bin_refinement reuses scores for identical bins (32% of 1,230) and records bins outside 200 kb-
		20 Mb as 0/100 without scoring (251 skipped, 0 passing bins lost). `--score-everything` disables the
		size filter. It now reports what its size filter removed.
	Changed - one conda env per workflow: 22 environments became 9. GUNC, DAS Tool, CoverM, fairy and Bakta
		are no longer opt-in; SemiBin2, VAMB and COMEBin share metawrap2-learned. 2.0.x env names stop
		working (aliases return in 2.1.2).
	Changed - the contig depth table is computed by metawrap2.depth (was jgi_summarize_bam_contig_depths):
		Pearson 0.9992 on means over 210,632 CAMI2 contigs.
	Changed - binning deletes per-sample BAMs, indexes and the aligner index once binners have their depth
		tables (`--keep-bams` keeps them). No module writes a SAM to disk; pipes run with `set -o pipefail`.
	Changed - blobology maps with minimap2 (was bowtie2) and takes `--aligner` (bwa, bwa-mem2); bowtie2 is
		no longer offered there. BAMs are no longer named <sample>.bowtie2.bam. read_qc still uses bowtie2.
	Changed - CONCOCT's numerical libraries run one thread per worker: 23.8 vs 29.9 cpu-hours, same bins.
	Changed - the very-fast tier's cost is 4.80 cpu-hours under minimap2 (was 22.9, a bwa-era figure).
	Changed - every command's help shows its default and accepted range (e.g. "[0-100] [default: 70]").
	Changed - rankings use clean high-quality MAG count with cost beside it, not MAGs per cpu-hour.
	Changed - docs consolidated into docs/; installation/, Module_descriptions.md and Usage_tutorial.md
		removed; WORK_SUMMARY.md is now docs/benchmarks.md.
	Changed - `benchmark score --checkm2` scores bins with CheckM2.
	Changed - the test suite pins use_conda_envs per test.
	Changed - none for assembly; MEGAHIT stays the default. metaSPAdes gave N50 12,874 vs 9,325 for 3.2x the
		cpu-hours (245 vs 77), 140 vs 15 GB peak memory, 197 vs 228 Mbp. `--metaspades` for contiguity.
	New - `binning --semibin2` (2.5.0), `--vamb` (5.0.4), `--comebin` (1.1.0), all seeded, installed by
		`metawrap2 install-env learned`. VAMB: `--minfasta` 200 kb, `-o ''`, `-m 250`. None can run on the
		tiny fixture.
	New - `binning --device {cpu,cuda,cuda:N,auto}` for the learned binners, default cpu; replaces
		`--semibin2-engine` and `--comebin-device`. GPU: VAMB 13.8x and SemiBin2 4.9x cheaper; COMEBin on CPU
		does not finish (~1,485 cpu-hours projected).
	New - `quant_bins --quantifier coverm` (genome mode, strobealign) and `--quantifier sylph`; units differ
		from salmon's. `--quantifier depth` reuses binning's depth table: 0.013 cpu-hours, Spearman 0.780.
	New - `bin_refinement --consolidator dastool` (DAS_Tool 1.1.7, needs `-a`, `--search_engine diamond`).
		Per-sample bins: 0.62 cpu-hours and 10 bins vs the metawrap consolidator's 4.52 and 13.
		`metawrap` stays the default.
	New - `metawrap2 cohort`: per-sample assembly, one coverage pass, per-sample binning, one dereplication.
		Adds coverage and dereplicate, drops reassemble_bins by default; `-j N` runs N samples per stage.
	New - `metawrap2 coverage`: cross-sample depth in N alignments, not N^2, via a prefixed contig catalogue
		(`-a LABEL=path`, `--separator`, default `~`). `binning --depth` bins from an existing table.
	New - `coverage --coverage fairy` (opt-in): 0.04 vs 2.26 cpu-hours but 27 vs 37 HQ MAGs and 76% vs 87%
		GUNC pass in B5; single-sample AMBER F1 0.212 vs 0.452.
	New - `metawrap2 dereplicate`: skani, `--ani 95`, `--min-aligned-fraction 10`, single linkage,
		representative is the best-scoring member. `--copy` copies instead of symlinking.
	New - `assembly --aligner` for hybrid-path remapping; default stays bwa. minimap2 is 8.8x cheaper but
		labels 4,113,486 reads unassembled vs bwa's 2,867,680.
	New - `reassemble_bins --max-coverage` thins each bin's reads to 50x (default); `--max-coverage 0` keeps all.
	New - `metawrap2 install-db checkm2`.
	New - `mw2`, the same CLI under a shorter name.
	New - `metawrap2 benchmark list|fetch|run|score` with datasets and comparisons in benchmarks/*.toml,
		a results table per dataset, a ~370 kB fixture, AMBER, CheckM, GUNC and abundance scoring.
		`--score-failed` scores crashed variants; `--reassess` re-runs assessors.
	New - benchmark B5: cross-sample coverage gives 37 HQ MAGs vs 32 single-sample, for 1.9 more cpu-hours
		of 90. Co-assembly is level at three samples: 85.1 cpu-hours and 81.4% GUNC pass vs 90.9 and 87.0%.
	New - coverage estimators compared per contig: CoverM reads 2.001x bwa because jgi filters below 97%
		identity.
	Fixed - MaxBin2 binned on depths `--noIntraDepthVariance` invented. Every earlier multi-sample MaxBin2
		result is affected. CAMI2: precision 0.6485 -> 0.8016, F1 0.4228 -> 0.4598, 188 bins -> 129.
	Fixed - quant_bins salmon tables were wrong with more than one bin (cumulative medians, NaN rows). Also:
		subfolders crashed it, .gz bins were refused, the two quantifiers labelled bins differently, and
		sample column order varied.
	Fixed - with one input bin set, metawrap_70_10_bins held every bin (376 where 178 pass 70/10).
	Fixed - unbinned.fasta was treated as a genome by bin_refinement, annotate_bins, classify_bins and
		reassemble_bins.
	Fixed - bin_refinement `--force` reruns failed at the end and kept the previous run's sets; `--resume`
		deleted the final bins it was resuming from. reassemble_bins had the same rerun defects.
	Fixed - reassemble_bins read filters used the NM tag, not FLAG: secondary/supplementary records became
		reads and a missing NM scored as perfect. The hybrid assembly now selects unmapped reads by FLAG 0x4.
	Fixed - `run` dropped learned binners before refinement, and any `--preset` but fast failed at refinement.
		Refinement now gets repeated `--bins`.
	Fixed - a co-assembly with no read_qc step had no reads; raw reads are now concatenated.
	Fixed - zero-length or all-N records broke samtools, VAMB and DAS_Tool; they are left out of working copies.
	Fixed - the depth samtools pipe could deadlock on unread stderr; the disk monitor could abort the wrong step.
	Fixed - CheckM1 lost its data path when its env was rebuilt; calls now set CHECKM_DATA_PATH from CHECKM_DB.
	Fixed - CheckM1 died with EOFError on deep output paths (unix-socket limit); a short symlinked temp dir is
		used, budget 75 characters. SemiBin2 uses the same helper.
	Fixed - a missing opt-in env or missing mamba is reported before the run, not at the first tool.
	Fixed - doctor/install-env: opt-in envs went unreported, shared envs passed "0/0 tools OK", `--lock`
		failed, GUNC's .dmnd was reported missing, opt-in names raised KeyError, `--force` never rebuilt.
	Fixed - env-wrapped commands set PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1, so ~/.local packages no longer shadow env pins.
	Fixed - an aligner crash in binning or reassemble_bins read as success (no pipefail).
	Fixed - a background run ignored SIGINT; an interrupt could orphan a running tool.
	Fixed - `--semibin2`/`--vamb`/`--comebin` help named env names that no longer exist.
	Fixed - benchmark harness: read_qc counts were one mate's; refinement arms dropped bin sets past three;
		crashed variants were scored; clean HQ was HQ x GUNC pass (COMEBin 158 -> 176); re-scores summed;
		CAMI2 contig names and reads_glob were mishandled; AMBER scored one file twice. Plus smaller fixes.
	Removed - `binning --subsample` and `coverage --subsample`: half the reads kept 33 of 37 HQ MAGs to save
		~1% of a run.
	Known issue - the identity floor divides by query length (`qlen`), not aligned length, so it is looser
		than asked under soft-clipping. Agrees with jgi at Pearson 0.9992; unchanged.
	Known issue - CONCOCT under Python 3.12 costs several times its Python 2.7 + MKL build (5.6 cpu-hours).
	Known issue - DAS_Tool fails on the 159 Mb remainder "bin" from a pooled gold assembly.
MetaWrap2 v=2.0.1
	Pipeline driver, verified resume, run history, one bin naming convention, one-command install, and
		fixes from an end-to-end run on 3 metaHIT gut metagenomes. No scientific behaviour changed.
	New - `metawrap2 run <sheet|directory> -o study/` runs a study from a TOML/YAML sheet: `-j`,
		`--resume`, `--dry-run`, `--steps`/`--skip`, `--no-coassembly`. `metawrap2 run --example` prints a sheet.
	New - `metawrap2 quickstart` builds and verifies every env (`--binning-only`, `--from-lock`). Databases
		download only with `--databases small|full|none`.
	New - `metawrap2 install-db` downloads, indexes and configures databases (`--list`, `--small`).
		`install-env -t N` / `install-db -t N` work in parallel.
	New - `metawrap2 config migrate <file>` converts an old KEY=/path config to TOML without executing it.
	New - --resume verifies: a step is skipped only if completed, outputs present and non-empty, inputs
		unchanged (manifest.json). `--strict-fingerprints` hashes whole files.
	New - `metawrap2 status <study>` (`--step`, `--audit`, `--json`); the audit also runs after every run.
	New - `metawrap2 history` (~/.metawrap2/history.jsonl) with filters and `--diff RUN RUN`.
	New - committed conda lockfiles (`install-env --lock`, `--from-lock`, `doctor --fix --from-lock`).
	New - provenance: run_environment.json, git commit and dirty state, database fingerprints, per-step peak
		RSS/CPU/wall. `metawrap2 calibrate` compares disk and memory estimates with real runs.
	New - inputs are checked for empty, malformed, truncated and bad-gzip files before tools run.
	New - SIGTERM/SIGHUP handled like Ctrl-C (exit 128 + signal); one writer per output directory (lock,
		`--force` overrides); `-m` is divided across `-j` workers.
	New - scratch_dir / $METAWRAP2_SCRATCH sets one scratch location; run log rotates at 256 MB, 3 backups.
	New - paired reads recognised as _1/_2, _R1/_R2, _R1_001/_R2_001, .1/.2; .gz and .bz2 throughout.
		read_qc gains `--single-end` and `--interleaved`.
	New - bin_refinement takes up to six bin sets (`--refine-combinations pairs-and-all|pairs|all`); two-
		and three-set output is byte-identical.
	New - `metawrap2 doctor` (= `metawrap2 test`), `--fix` rebuilds broken envs; also checks databases.
	New - opt-in tools wired up: `bin_refinement --checkm2`, `classify_bins --gtdbtk`, `annotate_bins --bakta`.
	New - progress bars on long loops (stderr, terminal only); retired names like `kraken` and --metabat1
		say what replaced them.
	Changed - bins are named bin_001.fasta for every binner (were bin.1.fa, bin.0.fa or CONCOCT ids); use
		*.fasta and `-x fasta`. Contents unchanged.
	Changed - .stats is now .stats.tsv and .contigs is .contigs.tsv.
	Changed - Python 3.10 minimum (was 3.8).
	Changed - --resume re-runs a step it cannot verify, so a resume may now do work that used to be skipped.
	Changed - the disk-space preflight warns instead of refusing; `strict_space_check = true` restores the stop.
	Changed - unknown [settings] keys are errors with a did-you-mean; unknown [databases] keys are notes.
	Changed - `-t` defaults to [settings] threads (was 1) and accepts `-t all`; Trim Galore gets `-j` (<=4).
	Changed - logging writes <output>/metawrap2.log; repetitive tool output is collapsed (1.8 MB -> 30 KB on
		a 3-sample run), `--verbose-logs` for raw output.
	Changed - pip-installable wheel with envs as package data; 456 tests (was 245) gate pushes; ruff
		findings 564 -> 0; mypy in CI.
	Fixed - helper scripts ran inside module envs without Python and failed (bin_refinement,
		reassemble_bins, quant_bins, blobology).
	Fixed - mamba 2.x choked on `--no-capture-output`; `metawrap2 test` reported every tool MISSING.
	Fixed - env yamls: blobology could not be created, classify_bins lacked blast, assembly pins python<3.12,
		bin_refinement pins setuptools<81; numpy/pandas/matplotlib/seaborn/scipy/tqdm declared.
	Fixed - metaBAT2 2.18 header annotations broke contig matching: refinement never merged metaBAT2 bins
		and quant_bins aborted. Contig id is now the header up to the first whitespace.
	Fixed - read_qc with `--skip-trimming --skip-bmtagger` moved the user's raw FASTQ; decompressed copies
		were left behind.
	Fixed - CONCOCT clusters were split at the first "." in contig names, scattering metaSPAdes contigs.
	Fixed - metaBAT2's non-genome outputs were scored as bins; now moved to metabat2_extra/. Its bins are
		renumbered numerically.
	Fixed - `--dry-run` stopped after one or two commands; `run --dry-run` executed the run.
	Fixed - history recorded every run as "completed" whatever the outcome.
	Fixed - kraken2 `--subsample` held all reads in memory; now reservoir sampling.
	Fixed - kraken2 taxonomy in prebuilt layouts, "superkingdom" renamed "domain", TAXATORTK_TAXONOMY_NCBI
		for binner/taxknife, BLAST v5 3-digit volumes (BLASTDB_NAME), relative `-o` paths.
	Fixed - the driver passed fixed -A/-B/-C to bin_refinement, failing when some binners were not selected.
	Fixed - dereplication crashed on a bin with no stats row; rm_short_contigs relied on SPAdes headers;
		MEGAHIT names read by position; sam_to_fastq wrote ">" headers.
	Fixed - `metawrap2 check` exits non-zero on a missing required database.
	Fixed - smaller issues: single-sample salmon dirs kept, blobplot bins matched nothing, needless bin
		rewrites, failed SPAdes reassembly now shows its errors, annotate_bins read sidecar files.
	Known issue - install-env cannot reach bakta, checkm2 or gtdbtk under any name; create them with
		`mamba env create -f <envs>/<name>.yaml`. Fixed in 2.1.2.
MetaWrap2 v=2.0.0
	Python-native successor to metaWRAP: same algorithms, tools and results as metaWRAP 1.x, no Bash,
		Perl or R, modern packaging and per-module conda environments.
	New - command is `metawrap2`; modules unchanged; `python -m metawrap2.modules.<module>` also works.
	New - per-module conda env "metawrap2-<module>", driven with mamba; `use_conda_envs=false` uses PATH.
	New - module commands are editable templates; constants overridable in ~/.metawrap2/config.toml.
	New - `metawrap2 check`, `metawrap2 install-env`, `metawrap2 test` (reports tool versions),
		`metawrap2 config init|show`, `metawrap2 completion bash|zsh`. One handler runs every external command.
	New - provenance per run: run_config.json, run_commands.txt, provenance.txt, run.stdout/run.stderr.
	New - global `--dry-run`, `--force`, `--resume` (state in <outdir>/.metawrap2/steps).
	New - warnings for paths with spaces or shell metacharacters; CI on Python 3.8-3.12, mkdocs site.
	Changed - configuration moved from the sourced config-metawrap to metawrap2.toml.
	Changed - blobology and Binning_refiner steps run in Python; flowcharts built by
		flowcharts/make_flowcharts.py.
	Removed - kraken1 module (use kraken2) and metaBAT1 binner (use metaBAT2).
	Changed - from metaWRAP 1.x: replace "metaWRAP" with "metawrap2"; see README "Coming from metaWRAP 1.x".

metaWRAP v=1.5 (superseded by MetaWrap2 2.0.0)
	General
		Python scripts now run under Python 3 (Python 2 reached end of life)
		sequence inputs can now be gzip- or bzip2-compressed (.gz/.bz2)
		added "check" command to verify tools and databases are available before a run
		dependencies loosened from old fixed versions and grouped by module under envs/
		clearer, tool-specific error messages when an external program fails
		reset the example database paths in config-metawrap to generic placeholders
	Read_QC
		gzipped read files (.gz) are now accepted as input
	Bin_refinement
		use a portable method to measure bin file sizes (works on macOS/BSD)
		bin consolidation is now deterministic regardless of input ordering
	Blobology
		bowtie2-build now uses the requested number of threads
		replaced the Perl and R helper scripts with Python; the contig annotation
			table and blobplot images are now generated with Python (no Perl/R needed)
	Reassemble_bins
		reassembly now uses one thread per bin as documented, reducing oversubscription

metaWRAP v=1.4 (in development)
	Kraken
		added new module "kraken2" for running kraken2 instead of kraken1 for read or assembly classification

metaWRAP v=1.3 (August 2020)
	Assembly
		fixed typo where megahit was not using the specified tmp directory
	Annotate_bins
		removed manual perl library handling feature
	Quant_bins
		bin coverage is now estimated with weighted median coverage (reduces contribution of outliers)
		fixed warning message to pring to stderr
		allow for periods in sample names
		allow for all 0 columns or rows
	General
		enforce MKL build of blas software to prevent CONCOCT infinite warning messages
		return correct error code instead of always returning 0
	Binning
		added SAM file cleanup step
		fixed library dependancy causing endless OPEN blas warning when running CONCOCT
		fixed assembly truncation bug after interrupted binning run
		fixed bug that left trailing characters in contigs of concoct bins, leading to refinement problems later
	Bin_refinement
		added additional checkpoints to prevent re-running checkm if program terminated before completing
		algorithm now ignores bins smaller than 50Kbp or larger than 20Mbp to save time
	Blobology
		fixed perl path to #!/usr/bin/env perl in scripts
	Kraken
		fixed syntax bug where some systems would misinterpret comments at the end of lines


